  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "Which of the following geometric series converge?\n\n- $-1+1-1+1-\u22ef$\n- $\\frac{4}{3}-1+\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{9}{16}+\u22ef$\n- $0.1+0.11+0.121+0.133\\,1+\u22ef$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's look at each series in turn.\n\n- For the geometric series $-1+1-1+1-\u22ef$ the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{-1} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\n- For the geometric series $\\frac{4}{3}-1+\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{9}{16}+\u22ef$ the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{(-1)}{(\\frac{4}{3})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$\n\n- For the geometric series $0.1+0.11+0.121+0.133\\,1+\u22ef$  the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{0.11}{0.1} \\\\  & =1.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent  because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only series II converges.",
